course: dictionary
lesson: 10317
title: One word, many meanings: trace
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word «trace» has more than one meaning, so context tells you which one to use. «A fossil is the remains or trace of a living thing from long ago kept in rock.» Here a trace is a small mark or sign left behind by something that lived long ago.
explain: «The past is gone, yet it leaves traces: memories, photos, fossils and old buildings.» Ask: what would fit here? Signs left behind by the past fit well, so a trace is something small that is left over from the past.
explain: Trace can also be an action. «Detectives trace a phone call» means they follow the signs to find where it came from. To trace a picture is to copy it by drawing over its lines through thin paper. The meaning changes, but the idea of following a line or a sign stays the same.
explain: Some phrases use trace in a set way. If someone vanishes «without a trace», they leave no sign at all behind them. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have chosen well.
fact: Trace can be a noun (a trace, traces) or a verb (trace, traces, traced, tracing).
fact: A trace is a very small amount or mark left behind.
fact: To trace something is to find where it came from or where it went by following signs.
fact: «Without a trace» means leaving no sign at all.
fact: Trace paper is thin paper you put over a picture to copy it by drawing over its lines.
fact: The correct phrase is «keep track», not «keep trace».
exercise: «The past is gone, yet it leaves traces: memories, photos, fossils and old buildings.» What are traces in this sentence? | *Small marks or signs left behind by the past | Loud noises from long ago | Machines that move by themselves | Brand new buildings
exercise: Detectives trace a phone call. What does trace mean here? | To draw over a picture on thin paper | To forget something completely | To buy something from a shop | *To follow the signs to find where it came from
exercise: Lily says her lost cat vanished «without a trace». What does that mean? | It came home with a bag of food | *It left no sign behind at all | It was found in the garden | It left a big mess everywhere
test: «Only a trace of blood was found on the floor.» What does trace mean in this sentence? | A bright colour | A large pool | A loud sound | *A tiny amount
test: Which phrase is correct for keeping up with what is happening? | trace back | *keep track | keep trace | keep tracing
exam: Which sentence uses trace to mean a small mark left behind? | Ella will trace the letters with her pencil | Ben traced the map onto thin paper | *Scientists found a trace of silk in an old grave | The police will trace the van by tomorrow
